# Day 5 – Database Models (Users, Resource Requests, Deployments)
## AWS EC2 Self-Service Provisioning Portal

---

## 1. What We Built

### Files Created/Updated

| File | Purpose |
|---|---|
| `app/models.py` | SQLAlchemy models defining the three database tables, matching the Day 3 schema design |
| `init_db.py` | Standalone script that creates all tables in the database (safe to re-run — never overwrites existing data) |
| `app/main.py` | Updated with a new `/db-check` endpoint to verify tables exist |

### Tables Defined (in `models.py`)

**`User`**
- `id`, `full_name`, `email` (unique), `password_hash`, `role` (`user`/`admin`), `created_at`
- Relationship: one user → many resource requests

**`ResourceRequest`**
- `id`, `user_id` (FK → users), `resource_type`, `workload_level` (`low`/`medium`/`high`), `instance_type`, `os`, `region`, `storage_gb`, `purpose`
- `estimated_cost`, `security_score` — filled in later by the Day 26–28 recommendation/cost/security modules
- `status` (`pending` / `approved` / `rejected` / `provisioning` / `provisioned` / `failed`)
- `reviewed_by` (FK → users, the approving admin), `created_at`, `updated_at`
- Relationships: belongs to one user, has one deployment

**`Deployment`**
- `id`, `request_id` (FK → resource_requests, unique — one deployment per request)
- `instance_id`, `public_ip`, `terraform_state_path`
- `deployment_status` (`provisioning` / `provisioned` / `failed` / `destroyed`)
- `error_message`, `provisioned_at`, `destroyed_at`
- Relationship: belongs to one resource request

**Design notes carried over from Day 3:**
- All three status fields use proper enums (not free-text strings) to prevent invalid values from ever being stored
- `destroyed_at` on `Deployment` supports the cost-control habit of destroying AWS resources between sessions
- `Deployment` is kept as a separate table from `ResourceRequest` so infrastructure metadata stays isolated from business/request data

